00:03:37.666 --> 00:03:40.100
Let's talk
more about that Disney dilemma, shall we?

00:03:40.116 --> 00:03:43.250
Basically, when you break it all down,
Disney's problems largely boil

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:46.916
down to three
major topics: streaming, budget and story.

00:03:46.916 --> 00:03:49.583
So let's go through them point by point.
Starting with streaming.

00:03:49.583 --> 00:03:53.056
One thing that everyone tends to overlook
when they first start a streaming service is that

00:03:53.056 --> 00:03:57.416
you need a steady flow of content to make it work,
to keep people hooked on the platform.

00:03:57.416 --> 00:04:01.516
And making content tends to be expensive,
hence the elongated merch push that

00:04:01.516 --> 00:04:02.450
you just went through.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:05.900
But even if you thread the needle
and managed to solve both of those issues,

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:08.550
making lots of content
for a relatively cheap price,

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:12.150
it just opens a well of new issues
that are more difficult to solve.

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:12.900
Let me explain.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.366
To prevent people from bouncing off
the platform, most of Disney's Star Studios

00:04:16.366 --> 00:04:19.733
have been relegated to content factories
to feed the Disney Plus machine.

00:04:19.733 --> 00:04:22.766
In 2020,
Disney held an Investor Day presentation

00:04:22.766 --> 00:04:25.800
where they had Marvel and Lucasfilm
announce ten projects each.

00:04:25.883 --> 00:04:27.600
Pixar announced another six.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.600
It wasn't all streaming focus,
but a whole lot of it was.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.016
In addition,
the service has been flooded with Disney

00:04:33.016 --> 00:04:36.833
dipping into all its other major IPs:
live action remakes of Peter Pan,

00:04:36.866 --> 00:04:40.366
serialized versions of High School
Musical, the return of nostalgic classics

00:04:40.366 --> 00:04:43.733
like Willow and the launch of anticipated
new IP like Artemis Fowl.

00:04:43.733 --> 00:04:44.700
Disney owns

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.450
some of the most beloved IP on the planet,

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:48.766
and they've been pulling it
all off the shelves to try and keep

00:04:48.766 --> 00:04:52.300
their content offering on Disney
plus robust and most importantly, sticky,

00:04:52.366 --> 00:04:55.883
to keep all those finicky streaming audiences
that are quick to cancel a subscription.

00:04:55.883 --> 00:04:59.816
Despite it being difficult and expensive
to stay on that online content treadmill,

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:00.750
at least on the surface,

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.866
it looks like Disney managed
to thread the needle. But in achieving

00:05:03.916 --> 00:05:07.650
that, they've actually expose themselves
to a problem that's far worse:

00:05:07.716 --> 00:05:08.783
brand dilution.

00:05:08.783 --> 00:05:09.983
There's no arguing that pretty much

00:05:09.983 --> 00:05:14.066
every single one of these cherished
franchises has become significantly

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.166
less special over the last

00:05:15.166 --> 00:05:18.566
five years, simply because there's
just so much of them around at this point.

00:05:18.566 --> 00:05:19.516
Toy Story four,

00:05:19.516 --> 00:05:21.500
Lightyear, Toy Story five,

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:24.833
you can only go to the pump so many times
before the audience can smell a shameless

00:05:24.833 --> 00:05:25.650
cash grab.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:26.933
Going back to those Investor Day

00:05:26.933 --> 00:05:30.016
announcements, most of the Star Wars ones,
they were quietly canceled.

00:05:30.016 --> 00:05:32.150
A completely different episode
nine, a Boba Fett

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:36.166
solo movie, a trilogy from Rian Johnson,
another trilogy from the Game of Thrones

00:05:36.166 --> 00:05:39.233
guys. When something new from Star Wars
gets announced, at this point,

00:05:39.233 --> 00:05:39.900
there's little trust

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:41.450
that it's ever going
to see the light of day

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:43.700
and even less trust
that it's going to be good.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:46.350
Andor, for instance,
it was legitimately great.

00:05:46.366 --> 00:05:49.666
Seriously, it was the single best thing
that Star Wars has produced in years.

00:05:49.766 --> 00:05:51.566
But you know why no one and watched it?
Because it was buried

00:05:51.566 --> 00:05:54.450
under a slew of underwhelming projects
like the Book of Boba Fett,

00:05:54.466 --> 00:05:56.666
Obi-Wan Kenobi, Late Season Mandalorian,

00:05:56.666 --> 00:05:58.883
and episode
nine. People lost trust with the brand

00:05:58.883 --> 00:06:01.700
and as a result, deprioritized
watching all the new stuff

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:04.916
that was rolling out. And the MCU?
It's having the exact same issue right now.

00:06:04.916 --> 00:06:08.700
Look at this: phase one had 746
minutes of content spread over five years,

00:06:08.866 --> 00:06:11.716
phase two 757 minutes spread over three years,

00:06:11.716 --> 00:06:16.133
and then you had phase three at 1500
minutes spread over another three years.

00:06:16.133 --> 00:06:19.616
That was a pretty significant jump,
but it was nothing compared to phase four.

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:20.183
Thanks in part

00:06:20.183 --> 00:06:23.400
to the introduction of Disney Plus
and the requirement for streaming content,

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:29.716
Phase four had a staggering
3500 minutes spread across only two years.

00:06:29.716 --> 00:06:34.883
There's been more MCU content in the last
two years than the preceding 12 combined.

00:06:34.883 --> 00:06:37.950
A Marvel release used to feel like
an event like you had to see it.

00:06:37.966 --> 00:06:40.516
Nowadays, though,
it's quite literally a weekly occurrence.

00:06:40.516 --> 00:06:42.166
So of course
people are going to be less excited

00:06:42.166 --> 00:06:45.766
for new installments like Secret Invasion,
regardless of their quality.

00:06:45.766 --> 00:06:48.233
Don't worry, we're going to address
the quality here in a second.

00:06:48.233 --> 00:06:48.866
What's worse,

00:06:48.866 --> 00:06:52.316
in their drive to Disney plus,
the mouse has lost out on a lot of money.

00:06:52.316 --> 00:06:52.966
They probably didn't

00:06:52.966 --> 00:06:56.266
need to. Looking at the list of original films
created and released by Disney,

00:06:56.266 --> 00:06:59.816
so many of them are going straight
to Disney plus without making any money

00:06:59.816 --> 00:07:04.550
in a theater. This year alone across all
their studios, six of the 14 Disney films

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:08.700
so far have premiered directly on Disney
Plus or Hulu, with no additional cost.

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:14.000
In 2022, a whopping 27 premiered on
streaming and only 16 were in theaters.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.416
Now compare all that to 2018, the year
before Disney Plus launched,

00:07:17.416 --> 00:07:21.200
where Disney released
only ten films total, all of which

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:22.583
obviously went to the theaters.

00:07:22.583 --> 00:07:25.400
Not only do these streaming premieres
mean that they're losing a lot of money

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:27.800
from the theatrical window,
but it becomes an even bigger issue

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:31.100
when you combine it with our next
major problem: budgeting. The budgets

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:34.166
for so many modern blockbusters
and especially those made by Disney,

00:07:34.166 --> 00:07:35.566
are just out of control.

00:07:35.566 --> 00:07:40.700
Indiana Jones and the Dial of Destiny
cost $300 million to make. Guardians three

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:43.400
and the little Mermaid,
$250 million each.

00:07:43.433 --> 00:07:48.900
Quantumania, a cool 200 mil. They even spent $150
million on Haunted Mansion.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.500
Why would you spend
$150 million on a Haunted Mansion movie?

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:53.266
And the true number

00:07:53.266 --> 00:07:56.700
is probably more like $300 million
if you include the marketing for it.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:59.716
Even Disney's animation
wing isn't safe. Both Encanto

00:07:59.716 --> 00:08:04.283
and Strange World cost somewhere between
$135 and $180 million to make.

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:05.750
While almost all of Pixar's

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:09.266
latest releases put their budgets
between $150 and $200 and range.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:12.116
Now, you might be looking at all those
numbers and thinking, Wow, that's a lot.

00:08:12.116 --> 00:08:13.966
But aren't all movies super expensive?

00:08:13.966 --> 00:08:15.716
And the answer is yes, but also no.

00:08:15.716 --> 00:08:17.450
Yeah,
they cost hundreds of millions of dollars,

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:19.800
but not that many hundreds
of millions of dollars.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:21.050
When you look at other massive

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:24.266
blockbusters currently in theaters,
most are not looking like this.

00:08:24.266 --> 00:08:27.200
Oppenheimer had a massive cast
and incredible experimental

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.883
filmmaking techniques, and it cost
just $100 million to make. John Wick four

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:35.183
elaborate action sequences with top tier
stunt work and incredible set designs

00:08:35.183 --> 00:08:39.150
at multiple real world locations
also cost just $100 million.

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:40.816
Barbie is the biggest movie of the summer.

00:08:40.816 --> 00:08:42.200
It has iconic art direction,

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:46.100
detailed fictional worlds with real sets
and A-list actors who enjoyed being there.

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:50.066
And it was made for less than $150
million. Over on the animation side,

00:08:50.066 --> 00:08:53.416
the newly released Teenage Mutant
Ninja Turtles movie cost $70 million

00:08:53.416 --> 00:08:55.883
while Puss in Boots,
The Last Wish, and Across the Spider-Verse,

00:08:55.883 --> 00:08:59.516
and the Super Mario Brothers
movie were all at about $100 mil each.

00:08:59.533 --> 00:09:04.016
All of these are half to a third of what
Disney is paying to make their movies.

00:09:04.016 --> 00:09:05.816
And it doesn't take a rocket scientist

00:09:05.816 --> 00:09:08.816
to understand that it's a lot easier
to get back what you put in

00:09:08.816 --> 00:09:13.000
when you're only $70 million in the hole
as opposed to $200 million.

00:09:13.016 --> 00:09:14.900
All of this begs
the question of why, though?

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:16.850
Why are these movies so expensive?

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:17.933
Well, it's complicated.

00:09:17.933 --> 00:09:18.900
And it ultimately comes down

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:22.500
to a lot of different factors:
an overreliance on CGI, a push to develop

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:25.516
new technologies, writing
that just doesn't keep budgets in mind.

00:09:25.516 --> 00:09:27.566
But one of the biggest contributors
to the inflation

00:09:27.566 --> 00:09:29.666
of a movie's budget over at Disney
that a lot of people

00:09:29.666 --> 00:09:31.366
don't talk about are reshoots.

00:09:31.366 --> 00:09:34.650
A reshoot is where a film brings back
the cast and crew to refilm scenes

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:35.400
that didn't work

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:38.850
or to film new scenes that become needed
as they get into the editing process.

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:39.500
Don't get me wrong,

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:41.933
reshoots are a perfectly normal
part of production

00:09:41.933 --> 00:09:43.650
and they can really help
bring a movie together.

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:46.066
A lot of very successful
films have had reshoots:

00:09:46.066 --> 00:09:46.883
Back to the Future,

00:09:46.883 --> 00:09:49.550
ET, Rocky that's not the sort of stuff
I'm talking about here.

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:52.950
What I'm talking about is when a movie
studio sees the rough cut of a movie,

00:09:52.966 --> 00:09:56.366
gets cold feet and then suddenly decides
they basically need to start over

00:09:56.366 --> 00:09:57.383
from scratch.

00:09:57.383 --> 00:10:00.766
Probably the most famous example of this
was with Zack Snyder's Justice League.

00:10:00.766 --> 00:10:02.816
But Disney,
they too have been guilty of this

00:10:02.816 --> 00:10:06.833
a lot lately.
Most publicly with Solo a Star Wars story.

00:10:06.866 --> 00:10:08.600
Originally,
that movie was directed by Phil

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:11.850
 Lord and Chris Miller, the two guys behind
Spider-Verse and The Lego Movie.

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:15.500
But Disney didn't like what they saw,
and so they hired Ron Howard to reshoot

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:19.250
an estimated 80% of the movie, thereby
doubling its budget

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:21.683
to somewhere around $300 million.

00:10:21.716 --> 00:10:24.166
Now, as we all know,
Solo did not do super well.

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:28.400
It only grossed about $400 million
worldwide, a number that suddenly becomes

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:32.900
a lot less painful to look at if
your original budget was just $125 million.

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:35.000
But all the streaming
and budgeting changes in

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:37.616
the world are going to be useless
if they continue to fail.

00:10:37.616 --> 00:10:38.450
At our third main

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:42.200
point: story. So many Disney films lately
have been failing at making a story

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:45.266
that's both worth telling
and that audiences want to hear.

00:10:45.266 --> 00:10:48.116
Disney keeps hoping
that you can still slap a brand name

00:10:48.116 --> 00:10:51.083
on to something, and it'll sell,
but that just doesn't work anymore.

00:10:51.083 --> 00:10:52.500
Audiences aren't stupid.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.433
They can tell when a story is being told
just because the bean counters

00:10:55.433 --> 00:10:57.866
think it's going to make money.
Like, who asked for Zootopia

00:10:57.866 --> 00:11:01.200
two, Frozen
three, Inside Out two, Toy Story five?

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:04.550
All of those are real movies
that are happening in the near future,

00:11:04.550 --> 00:11:06.950
even though it feels like that
list should be a parody.

00:11:06.983 --> 00:11:10.066
But making unnecessary sequels
is just the tip of the iceberg.

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:13.200
Disney also doesn't understand
where their stories fit anymore.

00:11:13.216 --> 00:11:16.350
Look no further than the Eternals,
which introduced a dozen new characters

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:19.516
and 10,000 years of back story
into a two hour film.

00:11:19.516 --> 00:11:23.033
Why wasn't this a Disney plus series
that could have given the concept time to breathe?

00:11:23.033 --> 00:11:27.083
Meanwhile, they're clearly stretching out
stories that don't need to be TV shows.

00:11:27.083 --> 00:11:29.183
The Obi-Wan series
is a great example of this.

00:11:29.183 --> 00:11:31.966
There was a really strong core
narrative there about Obi-Wan's

00:11:31.966 --> 00:11:33.116
relationship to Vader

00:11:33.116 --> 00:11:37.250
that ultimately got buried under
the bloatware of side quests and subplots.

00:11:37.316 --> 00:11:38.266
And hey, wouldn't you know it?

00:11:38.266 --> 00:11:39.616
Obi Wan was originally written

00:11:39.616 --> 00:11:43.583
to be a two hour film that they decided
to turn into a six hour series.

00:11:43.616 --> 00:11:47.000
If you want a super recent
example: Secret Invasion in which, man,

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:49.100
I have no idea what Marvel
was thinking on this one.

00:11:49.133 --> 00:11:52.666
Let's take this iconic storyline
from the comics with an Avengers level

00:11:52.666 --> 00:11:57.266
threat and adapted into a spy thriller
with basically no spying, no thrills,

00:11:57.266 --> 00:11:59.933
and no Avengers.
What should have been this amazing

00:11:59.966 --> 00:12:02.066
tentpole moment of the Avengers
line of films...

00:12:02.066 --> 00:12:03.650
I mean, who doesn't love themselves

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:06.916
a good impostor storyline... became yet
another piece of wasted

00:12:06.916 --> 00:12:10.433
potential content
to further dilute the MCU brand on Disney

00:12:10.433 --> 00:12:12.266
Plus. And again, here's the problem

00:12:12.266 --> 00:12:14.700
when you start
dumping out a lot of content all at once

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:18.200
just to keep us on the digital treadmill:
people start to see patterns.

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:20.566
They see the trends,
the formulas that you're using.

00:12:20.566 --> 00:12:23.850
For example, with so many Big Temple
Disney releases lately

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:27.116
had a main character becoming jaded
just because they lost a loved one.

00:12:27.116 --> 00:12:30.900
It happened with Han Solo, Luke Skywalker,
Indiana Jones, Obi-Wan Kenobi.

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:33.150
Heck,
even Haunted Mansion in Secret Invasion,

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:35.783
which you probably shouldn't know,
because why would you ever bother

00:12:35.783 --> 00:12:36.750
to see those things?

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:40.583
This generation of stories has seen
our fictional heroes become miserable,

00:12:40.583 --> 00:12:41.633
and let's be real,

00:12:41.633 --> 00:12:44.450
that is not something that fans
want to see.

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:46.650
We fell in love
with these characters for a reason.

00:12:46.666 --> 00:12:48.500
They inspired us for a reason.

00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:51.283
Seeing them crushed
under the same depression of reality

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:54.383
that we real people face
every day is not fun.

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:58.216
Sure, it might be more realistic
to see a disillusioned Indiana Jones,

00:12:58.216 --> 00:13:01.283
but audiences go to the movies
to escape from reality,

00:13:01.283 --> 00:13:03.866
to believe in something better,
something greater,

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:06.600
to have something to hope for
and aspire to.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:10.133
And Disney is no longer providing those
hopeful messages.

00:13:10.133 --> 00:13:12.383
So that's a lot of doom and gloom, right?

00:13:12.383 --> 00:13:13.800
Is there any way that Disney

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:17.033
and really the entire film
industry can fix all these issues?

00:13:17.033 --> 00:13:19.650
Well, yeah, actually. All you've got to do:
treat it like a YouTube channel.

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:21.566
What I mean to say
is that they need to program

00:13:21.566 --> 00:13:24.166
their slate of movies
just like you do a YouTube channel.

00:13:24.166 --> 00:13:26.300
I'm going to let you in on a little secret
here, loyal theorist,

00:13:26.333 --> 00:13:29.850
we spend a lot of time thinking about what
theories we put up across

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:32.100
all the different channels.
With multiple weekly meetings

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:34.700
trying to figure out what would work best
where, planning out

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:37.433
when things should go up,
and what videos complement each other,

00:13:37.466 --> 00:13:39.616
all for the overall health
of the business.

00:13:39.650 --> 00:13:41.183
You can't just throw up a video willy

00:13:41.183 --> 00:13:43.316
nilly and hope everything goes
well these days.

00:13:43.316 --> 00:13:45.716
No, you need to make sure
that you have solid videos here

00:13:45.716 --> 00:13:48.383
and there to keep the system
and your viewers happy.

00:13:48.383 --> 00:13:51.616
And everything we talked about today
with Disney are things that regularly

00:13:51.616 --> 00:13:55.583
come up in our day to day conversations
about how to run things here at THEORIST.

00:13:55.616 --> 00:13:57.000
First of all, we have a quality bar

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:00.116
that episodes just have to hit, otherwise
they're not going to get made.

00:14:00.116 --> 00:14:03.800
Our primary metric in the success
or failure of a video isn't revenue,

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:05.183
it's actually you, the community.

00:14:05.183 --> 00:14:08.416
Could we make more money
by pumping out more episodes every week? Probably.

00:14:08.416 --> 00:14:11.933
But in the process we would burn you out
and by necessity, dilute the show

00:14:11.933 --> 00:14:15.616
as we greenlight worse
and worse episodes. See in YouTube analytics,

00:14:15.616 --> 00:14:17.250
they give you a handy little chart ranking

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:20.633
your most recent releases from 1 to 10 in
terms of how well it's performing.

00:14:20.633 --> 00:14:23.266
Well, ideally, every upload
you do is going to be a one out of ten.

00:14:23.266 --> 00:14:26.216
It's just not realistic
to have that sort of expectation.

00:14:26.216 --> 00:14:29.216
In the end, something is inevitably
going to be a ten out of ten.

00:14:29.216 --> 00:14:30.266
And if you're good at what you do,

00:14:30.266 --> 00:14:32.566
you know when those nines and tens
are going to hit,

00:14:32.566 --> 00:14:34.850
and so you have to surround
that low performer

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:36.833
with stuff
that you know is going to do well.

00:14:36.833 --> 00:14:40.816
Otherwise, your risk taking L after L
after L and you start making a narrative

00:14:40.816 --> 00:14:43.666
about how bad your stuff is now,
how much it's fallen off.

00:14:43.666 --> 00:14:47.666
Or worse, the algorithm decides that
people no longer care about your channel.

00:14:47.666 --> 00:14:49.016
Apply this concept to Disney

00:14:49.016 --> 00:14:51.500
and you immediately
see what they have to do with their IP.

00:14:51.500 --> 00:14:53.416
When you have back to back
to back to back

00:14:53.416 --> 00:14:54.533
disappointments line up

00:14:54.533 --> 00:14:57.650
like in Marvel's recent roster,
it creates a downward spiral

00:14:57.650 --> 00:14:59.866
that's pretty hard to break
in the public consciousness.

00:14:59.866 --> 00:15:02.966
But if you mix in one or two goods
with the bad strategically,

00:15:02.966 --> 00:15:05.866
suddenly it's much harder
to craft a doom and gloom narrative.

00:15:05.866 --> 00:15:06.566
Here's an example

00:15:06.566 --> 00:15:10.400
programing lineup: Multiverse of Madness,
Wakanda Forever, Guardians three, Love

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:11.900
and Thunder, Quantumania.

00:15:11.900 --> 00:15:15.466
That looks a lot different
from what really happened where Guardians

00:15:15.466 --> 00:15:16.433
three appeared at the end.

00:15:16.433 --> 00:15:19.650
The real release order gave
you four disappointments in a row before

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:22.650
you got a small pick me up with
the generally well-received Guardians.

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:25.033
But if Guardians had been in the middle,
the disappointments

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:27.316
are suddenly broken up.
There's a gap there.

00:15:27.316 --> 00:15:28.650
The lackluster movies on either

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:32.000
side of it, it's just a franchise cost
and long waiting for the next big event.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:33.600
In fact, I can make this programing lineup

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:36.566
even better if Wakanda Forever
had actually been first in the list.

00:15:36.566 --> 00:15:40.583
The mediocre performance of Wakanda
actually was made significantly worse

00:15:40.583 --> 00:15:43.650
by the disappointment of Multiverse
of Madness that came directly before.

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:45.016
Had it come before Doctor Strange,

00:15:45.016 --> 00:15:48.533
that movie wouldn't have been saddled
with all the extra scrutiny of "Is Marvel

00:15:48.533 --> 00:15:50.516
losing its way?",
"if Wakanda Forever isn't good

00:15:50.516 --> 00:15:52.583
it means it's the end of Marvel," thereby
leading

00:15:52.616 --> 00:15:54.983
to a different cultural conversation
around it.

00:15:54.983 --> 00:15:56.250
Release order matters

00:15:56.250 --> 00:16:00.266
when it comes to shaping the narrative
around your brand, and it matters a lot.

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:03.150
The same thing
holds true for any experimental episodes.

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:07.250
Disney needs to be making films
about new IP like Pixar's Elio or lesser

00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:09.050
known characters
like Marvel's Moon Knight.

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:12.866
But obviously those projects are going to
be risky, so you balance that risk out by

00:16:12.866 --> 00:16:15.983
putting them between other projects
you expect to do well.

00:16:15.983 --> 00:16:18.600
Your Toy Story
five's, your Avengers, your Guardians.

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:20.966
This accomplishes
three things all at once:

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:22.916
you mitigate your risk,
you spread out the quote

00:16:22.916 --> 00:16:27.000
unquote cash grabs, and you potentially
find your next explosive IP.

00:16:27.016 --> 00:16:28.816
Again,
let's look at Film Theory for an example.

00:16:28.816 --> 00:16:29.416
On this channel,

00:16:29.416 --> 00:16:33.200
we have a few relatively sure things
our tentpole programing: analog horror,

00:16:33.233 --> 00:16:35.516
Spider-Man, nostalgic kid shows,
and knowing

00:16:35.516 --> 00:16:38.716
that we're able to put the stuff
that we're not sure about between them.

00:16:38.716 --> 00:16:41.750
And then if something surprises you
and it does manage to explode... BAM!

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:44.966
You have yourself a new strong subject
to slot other unknowns

00:16:44.966 --> 00:16:48.233
in between. That is literally
what happened to us with the Backrooms.

00:16:48.233 --> 00:16:51.650
We weren't sure that one was going to do
and it ended up doing gangbusters

00:16:51.650 --> 00:16:53.933
with people wanting more
and more of our analysis videos

00:16:53.933 --> 00:16:56.366
while they wear their new absurdly
comfortable theorywear

00:16:56.366 --> 00:17:00.166
hazmat puffer jacket. The gamble
paid off and it paid off in a big way.

00:17:00.166 --> 00:17:01.916
But we also know that we can't release

00:17:01.916 --> 00:17:04.500
too many of those things
in too quick of succession.

00:17:04.516 --> 00:17:06.166
We can't release a Backrooms theory

00:17:06.166 --> 00:17:08.633
every single week
because even though they'll do well,

00:17:08.633 --> 00:17:11.300
people will eventually stop caring
if we do too much

00:17:11.300 --> 00:17:13.633
and they'll stop coming back
because we're no longer adding

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:15.166
anything to the conversation.

00:17:15.166 --> 00:17:17.483
Does that sound familiar,
Star Wars? How about you, Marvel?

00:17:17.483 --> 00:17:18.050
Can you relate?

00:17:18.050 --> 00:17:19.966
And just in general,
when running a YouTube channel,

00:17:19.966 --> 00:17:21.233
you got to know what episode ideas

00:17:21.233 --> 00:17:24.083
are going to work best as shorts
and which ones merit a full video.

00:17:24.083 --> 00:17:26.116
We don't try to take something
that's clearly a short

00:17:26.116 --> 00:17:29.366
and then bloated into a full 15 minute
video or vice versa.

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:31.166
Just like Disney
shouldn't be looking at something

00:17:31.166 --> 00:17:35.000
that's clearly an hour and a half movie
and trying to turn it into a ten episode

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:38.266
miniseries just so they get more
watch minutes on their streaming app.

00:17:38.266 --> 00:17:41.416
Content needs to fit where it tells
the best story.

00:17:41.416 --> 00:17:43.933
Lastly, in terms of our budgeting,
we have to make sure that an episode

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:46.283
can make back the money
that we're spending on making it pay

00:17:46.283 --> 00:17:48.983
and the writers and editors
that are working on it and we budget it

00:17:48.983 --> 00:17:52.016
just based on the ad sense
we expect to make from that video.

00:17:52.016 --> 00:17:54.050
Anything extra
that we get from brand deals

00:17:54.050 --> 00:17:55.666
or merch sales or whatever is a nice plus.

00:17:55.666 --> 00:17:58.733
It allows us to expand the team
to do more ambitious projects,

00:17:58.733 --> 00:18:01.466
but we can't budget every single video
that way because eventually

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:04.366
we're going to lose that bet
and we can't run the business anymore.

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:06.983
Think of our AdSense like your box office,

00:18:06.983 --> 00:18:07.616
Disney. Sure,

00:18:07.616 --> 00:18:09.983
every now and then
we could do a more expensive video,

00:18:09.983 --> 00:18:13.133
something that takes a bit more time,
or just invest in a passion project.

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:17.183
That's going to be maybe a once or twice
a year thing, not an every release thing.

00:18:17.216 --> 00:18:21.616
Disney, you can't keep releasing $300
million movie after $300 million movie

00:18:21.616 --> 00:18:25.483
without knowing that you're going to earn
that back at the box office. Budget

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:27.916
according to what you know
you're going to earn in the theaters

00:18:27.916 --> 00:18:30.350
that way, all the ancillary stuff,
that's all gravy.

00:18:30.350 --> 00:18:32.783
Anything you earn on top of that,
boom, it's a bonus.

00:18:32.783 --> 00:18:34.466
You became that much more profitable.

00:18:34.466 --> 00:18:36.950
You know, it's going to help with that?
Fixing your reshoot issue.

00:18:36.983 --> 00:18:38.866
Let me tell you, as
I was researching this episode,

00:18:38.866 --> 00:18:41.866
just as someone running a media company,
my jaw hit the floor

00:18:41.866 --> 00:18:45.583
when I saw how often companies like Disney
are doing reshoots that majorly overhaul

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:46.100
the movie.

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:48.733
How are you rolling on day one
without having a clear vision

00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:52.166
that the writers, directors, producers
and studio have all agreed to?

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:52.700
At theorist

00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:55.400
we don't do an episode
unless all of our senior creative

00:18:55.433 --> 00:18:58.016
team are on the same page
about what that episode is going to be.

00:18:58.016 --> 00:19:00.000
And more importantly,
when we work with brands,

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:04.100
we require that every single stakeholder
review and lock the script

00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:05.550
before shooting begins.

00:19:05.550 --> 00:19:08.100
That way,
no one can ask for a last minute change.

00:19:08.100 --> 00:19:11.666
We learned early that those reshoots
and re-edits cost a fortune,

00:19:11.666 --> 00:19:12.750
killing our process.

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:15.300
But by forcing everyone
to get on the same page early,

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:17.266
even if it's painful and slows
the process down

00:19:17.266 --> 00:19:21.150
a bit at the beginning, it saves so much
time, money and a ton of headaches

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:24.750
on the other, much more expensive
end of the production process.

00:19:24.766 --> 00:19:26.983
All in all, in their desperate bid
to try to catch up

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:30.150
to Netflix with their streaming service,
throwing money at their problems in hopes

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:33.416
that they'll go away, Disney
lost sight of what made entertainment work

00:19:33.416 --> 00:19:35.550
since the very beginning:
making what you can,

00:19:35.566 --> 00:19:38.400
making what you're good at,
keeping it on budget and telling a story

00:19:38.400 --> 00:19:42.300
that's worth telling. And if we here on
YouTube can do it, they have no excuse.

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:45.016
Or if they can't get their act together,
even the death of Disney

00:19:45.016 --> 00:19:46.550
was ultimately for the best.

00:19:46.550 --> 00:19:47.516
But hey,

00:19:47.516 --> 00:19:49.366
remember,
go check out that new line of theory

00:19:49.366 --> 00:19:49.966
wear. Again,

00:19:49.966 --> 00:19:51.766
I cannot stress enough
just how much work went

00:19:51.766 --> 00:19:54.233
into all these items
because we wanted this to be more

00:19:54.233 --> 00:19:57.350
than just your typical YouTuber merch,
like, well, just slap the logo on it.

00:19:57.350 --> 00:20:01.166
No, these are actual clothing items
that have high quality fabric expert

00:20:01.166 --> 00:20:03.916
details and stuff that's going to make you
feel incredible when you wear it.

00:20:03.916 --> 00:20:04.883
So please do me a favor:

00:20:04.883 --> 00:20:06.566
go check out all the cool stuff
that we made

00:20:06.566 --> 00:20:09.833
by heading on over to our theory wear
website links in the description below.

00:20:09.833 --> 00:20:12.150
Or maybe it's on screen right now.
I don't know.

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:14.483
Maybe through the magic of editing
we can make that happen.

00:20:14.483 --> 00:20:18.200
And as always, my friends, until next
time, remember, it's all just a theory,

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:35.833
a Film Theory! And cut.

